WEBVTT

00:00:00.071 --> 00:00:03.200
A practical CTI workflow starts with the decision.

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:06.715
Before collecting, ask who needs the intelligence and what they need to decide or do.

00:00:06.715 --> 00:00:08.435
A leader may need risk implications.

00:00:08.435 --> 00:00:10.675
A SOC analyst may need triage guidance.

00:00:10.675 --> 00:00:13.360
A detection engineer may need behavior and telemetry.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:16.230
A vulnerability manager may need exploitation context.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:19.500
Defining the audience keeps the product from becoming a generic report.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:21.360
Next, set intelligence requirements.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:24.590
A good requirement is specific enough to guide collection and analysis.

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:28.530
Then collect from appropriate sources: internal telemetry, incident reports,

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:32.920
malware reports, vulnerability advisories, vendor reporting, government advisories,

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:36.480
ISAC or community sharing, open-source reporting, and threat research.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:37.890
More sources are not the goal.

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:41.440
Relevant, reliable, timely, and properly handled sources are the goal.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:44.279
Process and normalize the material so it can be analyzed.

00:00:44.279 --> 00:00:49.365
Deduplicate indicators, separate facts from claims, map behavior to ATT&CK where useful,

00:00:49.365 --> 00:00:51.860
check recency, and preserve source restrictions.

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:56.005
Analysis should produce a judgment that explains relevance, confidence, assumptions,

00:00:56.005 --> 00:00:57.410
and defensive implications.

00:00:57.410 --> 00:01:01.005
Use careful language: observed, reported, assessed, likely, possible,

00:01:01.005 --> 00:01:03.015
and confirmed all mean different things.

00:01:03.015 --> 00:01:07.075
As a review habit, ask three questions before sharing: What decision does this support?

00:01:07.075 --> 00:01:08.800
What evidence supports the judgment?

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:10.610
What action should the audience take next?

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:13.410
If any answer is vague, the product probably needs more work.

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:15.555
That does not mean waiting for perfect certainty.

00:01:15.555 --> 00:01:19.095
It means being clear enough that the consumer can act without guessing what the analyst

00:01:19.095 --> 00:01:19.640
intended.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:21.670
Disseminate the result in the right format.

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:25.120
That may be an executive summary, detection story, incident response note,

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:28.655
vulnerability ticket, watchlist, blocklist, or hunting hypothesis.

00:01:28.655 --> 00:01:32.780
Include enough context for action and enough handling information to protect sources,

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:34.430
privacy, and sharing agreements.

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:38.560
Then operationalize the intelligence by assigning owners, integrating it into workflows,

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:39.680
and tracking outcomes.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:43.318
For a beginner CTI analyst or security engineer, the checklist is simple:

00:01:43.318 --> 00:01:47.770
define the decision, identify the audience, write the requirement, choose sources,

00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:52.015
process the data, analyze with confidence, package for the workflow, drive action,

00:01:52.015 --> 00:01:53.200
and ask for feedback.

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:54.714
Threat intelligence is not a feed.

00:01:54.714 --> 00:01:58.820
It is a disciplined process for turning threat information into defensive action.

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:02.960
Keep the beginner mindset practical: be specific, cite sources, mark confidence,

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:06.300
respect handling rules, and ask whether the intelligence changed anything.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:10.170
If it changed a decision or improved a defensive workflow, it produced value.

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:13.380
If it did not, use feedback to make the next product sharper.
